Elex is a game which will divide the opinion of players. It has unique charm which contains strong rpg elements which are hardly found in the modern games. And I love it. It is rare type of game in the market. Players used to the modern rpg games may not love it. Moreover, there are some balancing and placing issues making the early game extremely long and uninteresting. 

Therefore, I only recommend this game to rpg fans who can embrace the flaws of the game and don't mind the hard old school elements in the game.

Pros:
+ Decent Size of Open World; 
+ Strong RPG elements;
+ Hard Difficult than Average Games;
+ Challenging Combat System;
+ Decent Play Hours;
+ Decent Number of Quests;
+ Rare Theme in the Game Market (Little bit Sci-fi, Fallout and Skyrim);
+ Replayability is high due to different faction you choose;

Cons
- Early 8-15 hours gameplay is boring (dialogue and fleeing simulator);
- Many dialogues BUT most of them are lengthy and boring;
- Graphic is hit and miss;
- Highly reusing npc (female) model and awkward facial expression; 
- Combat is clunky and not much surprise later on;
- Target system of the game is bad to multi enemies;
- Unbalance difficulty of enemies (too high early on & not much challenging from mid game);
- Unbalance exp point (exp of killing wild animals is too low compare that of doing quests);
- Stats are high for weapon;
- Many weapons BUT you can't use most of them in one playthrough;  


The followings are some annoying features in the game you may need to know before the purchase:

1) Long and Boring Start (Many players rage-quit the game at this point)
At the beginning of the game, you will find yourself hard to kill even the weakest enemies.  Avoiding those enemies is the only option at the beginning of the game. You need to complete quests to level up. At one point, you will find yourself can kill certain enemies without dying so much.

However, the dialogues of quests (or most of the quest)  in the beginning area are lengthy and lifeless. Some dialogues like filler talk about miscellaneous things rather than gives you more information, or the important events occurring in the world.  You will expect to spend hours on the dialogue which you can't avoid. For me, I spent almost 12 hours in the early part without much combat involvement. And I really need a break after the arrival of the 2nd town before the lengthy conservation again. Once you reach the point you can combat outside. You don't need to avoid those creatures again and don't need to focus only on those quests, the game becomes shinning.  

2) Clunky melee combat and some unfair balanced issues
Melee combat in Elex is work but not great. Some aspects of the combat are brilliant but many of them are annoying. Although it also involves magic or range combat. However, a fully magic or range gameplay is expensive and you need to spend the money in armor and weapon. 

For the good part, the combat in Elex is more challenging than most of the modern games. It involves Dark Souls's Stamina System to keep you tracking and managing your stamina. No button mashing attack or infinite dodge/ rolling are promoted. 
It also involves the Combo System, more damage will be rewarded for consecutive attacks. It makes you have to learn the right timing to use your combo. Not simple hit and roll strategy to be used entirely time. 

Combat experience is ruined a little bit by the clunkiness and the unfair balance of rewarding between the quests and the mobs in wildness. Attacks in Elex feel delayed. It is hard to accept at the start when you played some great action modern games like Dark Souls series. Moreover, the combat feels unfair when facing multiple enemies. The target lock system is not fluent enough to focus the enemies you want. Also, some battles feel unfair when they put large amount of ranged enemies which some of them will stun-lock you in the battle. I feel cheating when run for a long distance to pull a few enemies away from the others. 

Besides, non-mission-based battles feel unrewarded. No matter how many times and tries to spend on the combat. The rewarding for non-mission-based battles largely underestimated when compared to quests. Average quests which taken less time to complete will reward you more when compare to non-mission combats. Therefore, sometimes you will doubt is there a reason for the combats.

If you can bear the above flaws on the game, then you may find a rare open world RPG which gives you challenges and keep you busy for a pretty decent period. I brought the game on sales, it provided me a decent time of gameplay. Therefore, I recommend this game to RPG lovers, especially to those enjoy the old school type RPG lovers. (Disclaimer: I almost quitted the game in the first 20 hours).

